<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Niebla</h1><p class="st-meta">1023 CE – 1053 CE (approx.) · capital Niebla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A taifa based on the walled town of Niebla on the Tinto river, ruled by the Arab Banu Yahya until Seville annexed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-yahya-al-yahsubi">Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi</a>, of an Arab family long settled in the region, established an independent principality around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niebla">Niebla</a>, taking advantage of the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and the rivalries of the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niebla">Niebla</a> controlled the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-tinto">Tinto</a> valley and the mining district inland, with its red stone walls and fertile riverland. Its rulers, styled with honorifics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>, were caught between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a>, and briefly accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville's</a> protection before resisting it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1053 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid-of-seville">al-Mutadid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niebla">Niebla</a>, deposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yahya-al-yahsubi">Muhammad ibn Yahya</a>. The town later reappeared twice as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-niebla">Second Taifa of Niebla</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-taifa-of-niebla">Third Taifa of Niebla</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-niebla.jpg" alt="Taifa of Niebla" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Niebla.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1023 CE – 1053 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Niebla</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi, Muhammad ibn Yahya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Niebla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=taifa-of-niebla" aria-label="Taifa of Niebla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4513 833 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Niebla lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4833 1089L4850 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4833 1089h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4833 1089h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Niebla. <a href="/atlas?year=1038&amp;focus=taifa-of-niebla">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

